1
00:00:05,321 --> 00:00:13,969
On 22 July 1992, Pablo Escobar, the most
notorious prisoner in Colombia, escaped
from his own prison.

2
00:00:15,321 --> 00:00:20,689
It stood on a hillside above Medellín, on
land he owned, and its guards were said to
be his.

3
00:00:21,901 --> 00:00:27,269
This is how a surrender became a hideout,
and how a state lost its most wanted
prisoner.

4
00:00:33,661 --> 00:00:39,249
By 1989, Escobar's Medellín cartel was at
war with the Colombian state.

5
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:50,929
Frontline's 1995 film described car bombs,
the murder of police officers and
journalists, and the shooting of a
presidential candidate, Luis Carlos Galán.

6
00:00:52,341 --> 00:00:58,829
Frontline said a bomb outside the
headquarters of the DAS security service
killed sixty-three people and wounded six
hundred.

7
00:01:00,281 --> 00:01:02,715
The government formed an elite force to
hunt him.

8
00:01:03,067 --> 00:01:04,479
The raids kept missing.

9
00:01:05,191 --> 00:01:08,969
Once, Frontline said, they came so close
that his bed was still warm.

10
00:01:31,161 --> 00:01:35,169
What Escobar feared most was extradition
to the United States.

11
00:01:36,621 --> 00:01:44,649
In 1991, a Constituent Assembly was
rewriting Colombia's constitution, and it
voted against the extradition of Colombian
nationals.

12
00:01:46,121 --> 00:01:49,928
On 19 June 1991, Escobar surrendered.

13
00:01:51,421 --> 00:01:54,499
Radio first reported the surrender as
unconfirmed.

14
00:01:55,011 --> 00:01:58,409
Then it confirmed it, with helicopters
landing at the prison.

15
00:02:26,861 --> 00:02:34,869
Frontline said the deal followed six
months of secret talks, and that a
government helicopter found him waiting at
a house above Medellín.

16
00:02:36,441 --> 00:02:44,489
A Chilean television report on the
surrender shows Escobar on camera, a
helicopter over the hills, and a priest
beside the road.

17
00:03:22,141 --> 00:03:26,048
The prison was called La Catedral, the
cathedral.

18
00:03:27,641 --> 00:03:32,448
According to Frontline, it was built on
land Escobar owned, to his own designs.

19
00:03:34,041 --> 00:03:36,617
Some called it Club Medellín.

20
00:03:36,367 --> 00:03:40,769
The guards joked that it was not maximum
security, but maximum comfort.

21
00:03:42,341 --> 00:03:49,469
A DEA agent, Stephen Murphy, told
Frontline that Escobar had handpicked the
guards and paid them a monthly stipend.

22
00:03:51,022 --> 00:03:54,728
After the escape, an American television
crew filmed the compound.

23
00:03:56,361 --> 00:04:04,229
Its raw tape shows a guard tower, a gym, a
billiard table, a chapel, a jukebox, and a
bedroom with a view of the city.

24
00:04:05,821 --> 00:04:13,109
Frontline described a suite with a
jacuzzi, a bar, weekly parties, and a
telescope aimed at the building where his
family lived.

25
00:04:14,581 --> 00:04:22,029
Frontline said Escobar had brought four of
his own lieutenants to the prison to
torture and murder them, over a dispute
about money.

26
00:04:23,701 --> 00:04:32,449
Semana reported that on 21 July, the
government said it had evidence of
kidnapping, torture and killing inside La
Catedral.

27
00:04:39,041 --> 00:04:44,649
The next day, 22 July, officials came to
move him to a more secure prison.

28
00:04:46,241 --> 00:04:51,889
Semana reported that he held the vice
minister of justice, Eduardo Mendoza, in
his cell.

29
00:04:53,420 --> 00:05:02,989
A United States Embassy report of 11
August said there was a shootout at the
prison, and that Escobar and his brother
had reportedly disappeared into its tunnel
network.

30
00:05:04,401 --> 00:05:13,589
The same report said a statement
attributed to the Extraditables blamed
President Gaviria for the incident and
threatened the United States Embassy.

31
00:05:15,042 --> 00:05:18,148
Frontline's film played a radio bulletin
about his refusal.

32
00:05:31,261 --> 00:05:38,168
Frontline said soldiers surrounded the
prison, but Escobar had bribed so many
officers that he simply walked out the
back gate.

33
00:05:39,840 --> 00:05:47,800
The same embassy report called the botched
transfer a correct decision executed, in
its words, with unbelievable incompetence.

34
00:05:48,351 --> 00:05:55,410
It said poor planning and flawed policies
played a part in the escape, but that
corruption was the determining factor.

35
00:05:56,841 --> 00:06:01,264
So the accounts differ: a tunnel network,
a back gate, or bribed officers.

36
00:06:01,576 --> 00:06:02,989
None of them is confirmed.

37
00:06:04,441 --> 00:06:12,810
On 23 July, the White House press
secretary said Escobar had escaped when
Colombian authorities tried to move him to
a more secure facility.

38
00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:21,799
On 31 July, a United States Embassy cable
quoted a letter from President César
Gaviria.

39
00:06:21,549 --> 00:06:30,390
He said he would use all means of
international cooperation to capture
Escobar, and expected to find neither
complacency nor complicity with organized
crime.

40
00:06:36,801 --> 00:06:40,208
The hunt that followed lasted 498 days.

41
00:06:41,741 --> 00:06:47,029
Frontline said forces carried out eleven
thousand search warrants and set up four
thousand roadblocks.

42
00:06:48,541 --> 00:06:56,509
Callers to the American Embassy
switchboard warned that efforts to capture
Escobar would cause, in their words, a lot
of American blood to flow.

43
00:06:57,880 --> 00:07:03,578
By the start of September, the Christian
Science Monitor counted about one hundred
American agents in the search.

44
00:07:04,049 --> 00:07:11,250
General Joulwan, of the United States
Southern Command, flew in on 15 September
to discuss it, a cable said.

45
00:07:12,881 --> 00:07:21,789
In December 1992, an embassy cable said
Colombian commanders were being kept on
duty through the holidays, with the hunt
for Escobar among their operations.

46
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:31,461
A group calling itself Los Pepes, people
persecuted by Pablo Escobar, began
attacking his properties and his lawyers.

47
00:07:32,212 --> 00:07:37,709
In 2008, the National Security Archive
published declassified cables about the
group.

48
00:07:39,341 --> 00:07:46,109
On 2 December 1993, in Medellín, the hunt
ended, 498 days after the escape.

49
00:07:47,681 --> 00:07:51,808
Accounts of how he died still differ, and
we have chosen not to show it.

50
00:07:53,261 --> 00:08:00,829
For 498 days, Colombia's forces and
American agents searched for a man who had
once been in their custody.

51
00:08:02,381 --> 00:08:05,448
The state locked him inside a prison built
to his terms.

52
00:08:07,022 --> 00:08:09,428
So, who was really holding whom?
